---
// Slot order: Claim · Evidence · Explore (none yet) · Limits · Cite.
// This project is an Investigation: the finished work is the source audit, not a framing analysis.
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import Claim from '../../../components/Claim.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import HallyuCoverage from '../../../components/figures/HallyuCoverage.astro';
import hallyu from '../../../data/hallyu.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'hallyu-indian-press')!;
---
<Project title="Looking for Hallyu in the Indian press"
  description="A source audit and corpus-development project on Indian English-language newspaper coverage of Korean popular culture, 2000–2026. The national press was nearly silent on Hallyu before 2011, and later counts are confounded by database coverage.">
  <div class="wrap">

    <Claim project={p}
      meta={`${hallyu.korea_baseline_2000_2010.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korea-related articles in licensed full text, 2000–2010 · ${hallyu.hallyu_term_uses_2000_2010} use of “Hallyu” or “Korean wave” in that period · pilot corpus ${hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs} documents`} />

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Evidence I</span>An absence, corroborated twice</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          During the decade in which Korean popular culture was spreading through Northeast India, the
          English-language national press was nearly silent about it. Licensed full-text search across the
          Times of India, Hindustan Times and Indian Express finds
          {hallyu.korea_baseline_2000_2010.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korea-related articles in 2000–2010, and
          the words “Hallyu” or “Korean wave” appear exactly {hallyu.hallyu_term_uses_2000_2010} time in the
          whole period: a single incidental wire-story use in 2007. Only 58 articles match the project's
          broader candidate vocabulary before 2011, almost all in film-review contexts.
        </p>
        <p>
          A parallel open-web harvest of The Hindu's daily section indexes for the same decade, enumerated
          through the Internet Archive, recovered {hallyu.pilot.hindu_headline_matches} Korea-related headlines
          and a {hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs}-document pilot corpus, and zero culture-context Hallyu headlines.
          Two independent routes to the same decade return the same absence.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Apparatus label="Why this is a finding and not a gap">
        <p>
          The sources were searched, the criteria are stated, and the retrieval was validated against licensed
          full text. A documented near-zero under stated criteria is evidence about what the press was doing.
          It is distinct from the 2000–2004 window below, where no licensed source exists at all and the
          correct description is unvalidated.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Evidence II</span>The curve cannot be read as popularity</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Counts of Korean-culture articles do rise steeply after 2011. They cannot yet be interpreted,
          because the thing being counted changes underneath them. Each database begins its full text in a
          different year, between 2005 and 2015, and from 2017 all of them start indexing online-only editions
          alongside print. A count that mixes those objects will rise whether or not any newspaper published
          more.
        </p>
      </div>
      <HallyuCoverage />
      <Apparatus label="A rule this project holds itself to">
        <p>
          The phrase “Hallyu coverage increased” is not used in this project, and variables are named so that
          Korean-popular-culture counts and Hallyu-vocabulary counts can never be silently conflated. Print
          objects are separated from online objects before any comparison, and counts are reported per
          newspaper per year rather than as a single national curve.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Method</span>How the corpus is being built</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A three-stage pipeline — discover, harvest, build — enumerates archive discovery pages, fetches
          candidates cache-first at polite rates, and compiles a full-text-searchable corpus. Every HTTP
          response is cached permanently, so re-running any stage never re-touches the network. Vocabulary
          matching is deliberately two-tier: a broad Korea net for recall, then the candidate Hallyu vocabulary
          for precision, evaluated against licensed full-text counts.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full 2000–2026 corpus is deliberately not yet collected. Collection phases are gated on the
          validation conditions the audit set, and the framing analysis, with its codebook and two-coder
          reliability test, is the stage after that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Limits</span>What this project does not claim, and what it will publish</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul class="limits">
          <li>
            <b>No framing analysis exists yet.</b> Everything on this page is a count or a statement about
            source availability. No frame, topic or evaluation coding has been applied to a validated sample.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>2000–2004 is unvalidated.</b> No licensed full-text source at the host institution covers it,
            and web-archive coverage of that window is thinnest.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The pre-2011 absence still awaits recall validation</b> against a historical database before it
            can be published as a strong claim.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Newspaper text is copyrighted and will never be republished here.</b> What will be published:
            counts by newspaper and year with print and online separated; the candidate vocabulary and the
            relevance decision tree; the source-coverage audit; and the staff, wire and unknown provenance
            shares, which are a core variable of the design.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">
          Sources, ethics, pipeline and citation are on the
          <a href="/projects/hallyu-indian-press/data/">data page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Project>
<style>
  .limits { max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .limits li { margin-bottom: 12px; }
</style>
